+1.1 For $k\ge0$, $n\ge1$ and each word $w\in\mathcal A^n$, define $q_{k,n}(w)$ on each positive-probability atom of $\sigma(X_{-k},\ldots,X_{-1})$ as the conditional average of $1_{\{X_0^{n-1}=w\}}$ there; on null atoms use the uniform vector on $\mathcal A^n$. Refining the past partition shows that for fixed $n,w$, $(q_{k,n}(w))_{k\ge0}$ is a martingale bounded in $[0,1]$. For rational $0\le u<v\le1$, hold one unit after the martingale first falls to at most $u$, sell it when the martingale next reaches at least $v$, and repeat. Each finite-horizon gain is a sum of predictable holdings times martingale increments, so its expectation is zero by the martingale property. Every completed upcrossing earns at least $v-u$, and an unfinished holding loses at most one because the martingale stays in $[0,1]$. Thus the expected completed upcrossing count through any horizon is at most $(v-u)^{-1}$. The counts increase with the horizon, so [F4] gives finite expected total count and hence finitely many total upcrossings almost surely for each rational pair. A bounded real sequence that does not converge has distinct liminf and limsup, hence has infinitely many upcrossings of some rational interval strictly between them. A countable intersection over rational pairs, words and lengths therefore gives simultaneous almost-sure limits $q_{\infty,n}(w)$. [F4, given, algebra]
 
