+2.1 For each $n$, the limits are nonnegative and sum to one because there are finitely many words and every $q_{k,n}$ is a probability vector. They are $\mathcal F^-$-measurable. Every finite-past cylinder $B$ belongs to the conditioning sigma-algebra for all sufficiently large $k$; [F5], with majorant one on the probability space, then gives $\int_Bq_{\infty,n}(w)\,dP=P(B\cap\{X_0^{n-1}=w\})$. For fixed $n,w$, the finite measures $B\mapsto\int_Bq_{\infty,n}(w)\,dP$ and $B\mapsto P(B\cap\{X_0^{n-1}=w\})$ agree on the algebra of finite-past cylinders. Their agreement class is a monotone class by continuity of finite measures, so [F3] extends the identity to every $B\in\mathcal F^-$. Thus $q_{\infty,n}(w)$ is a conditional probability version of the block given the full past. [F3, F5, step 1.1, given]
 
 2.2 Fix a positive-probability atom $B$ of the $k$-past partition. Applying [F2] to $B$ and its successive intersections with the realized symbol events factors $P(X_0^{n-1}=w\mid B)$ into ratios on the refined atoms $B\cap\{X_0^{i-1}=w_0^{i-1}\}$. Those ratios are exactly the finite-past conditional probabilities $p_{k+i}(w_i)$, since the refined atom records $X_{-k}^{i-1}$. Therefore, almost surely on the realized block, $$q_{k,n}(X_0^{n-1})=\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}p_{k+i}(X_i).$$ For fixed $n$, every factor converges as $k\to\infty$ to $p_\infty(X_i)$ evaluated at the shifted path; the left side converges to $q_{\infty,n}(X_0^{n-1})$ by step 1.1. The realized one-symbol limits are positive almost surely by [F1] and stationarity, so $$-\log_2q_{\infty,n}(X_0^{n-1})=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}g_\infty\circ T^i\quad\text{almost surely}.$$ [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
 
